Build a Hive Desktop applet.
{{template "app" .}}
An applet is a UI that opens over the app, from the command palette or a key the
user binds. It is either declared in YAML and drawn by Hive, or a whole web page
you write. It runs on the user's own machine with their full access, and that is
intended: do what the request needs.

Write the applet to {{.Env.AppletsDir}}/<id>/applet.yaml. The directory name is
the applet's id: a slug of lowercase letters, digits and hyphens. Put any script
it needs in the same directory and call it through `$HIVE_APPLET_DIR`. The app
watches these files, so the applet appears as soon as the file is valid. Nothing
runs until the user opens it.

Pick the view from what the user asked for:

- `table` is a large overlay for watching and managing things. Rows can refresh
  on a timer. Keys run actions against the selected row.
- `picker` is a compact fuzzy finder, like telescope in Neovim. The user types to
  filter and Enter runs the first action, then the picker closes.
- `html` is your own page, for anything those two cannot be: charts, games, editors,
  dashboards, or a look of its own. Prefer it whenever the request goes beyond a list.

## Schema

Unknown keys are errors.

- `version`: must be `1`.
- `title`: the name in the palette and the overlay header.
- `view`: `table`, `picker`, or `html`.
- `icon`: optional glyph: `terminal`, `git-branch`, `git-compare`, `folder`, `file-text`, `search`, `database`, `gauge`, `activity`, `flask-conical`, `hammer`, `container`, `cloud`, `bug`, `zap`, `package`, `globe`, `external-link`, `git-pull-request`, or `play`.
- `cwd`: where commands run. Omit it when the applet is about the code in front of the user: commands then run in the on-screen session's checkout, and the applet is only offered while a session is on screen. Set `"~"` or an absolute path when the applet works anywhere. A relative path is relative to the applet's directory.
- `data`: where the rows come from.
  - `command`: run through `sh -c` for up to 30 seconds. Failure shows its stderr in the applet.
  - `format`: `json` (default) reads one JSON array of objects, or one JSON object per line. `lines` makes each line a row with the single field `line`.
  - `refresh`: optional duration such as `5s`, at least `1s`, to re-run while the applet is open.
- `columns`: at least one, in display order. The filter matches their text.
  - `key`: the field in each row. Nested objects are not traversed, so flatten them in the command.
  - `label`: optional header, defaulting to `key`.
  - `tones`: optional map from a value to a badge tone: `neutral`, `success`, `accent`, `muted`, or `danger`.
- `actions`: what the user can run against the selected row. A `picker` needs at least one.
  - `id`: a slug, unique in the file.
  - `label`: the button text.
  - `run`: the shell command.
  - `mode`: `background` (default) runs through `sh -c` for up to 5 minutes, shows the result, then refreshes the rows. `terminal` opens the command in the pop-up terminal, for anything long-running or interactive.
  - `key`: optional single lowercase letter or digit that runs it in a `table`. Not `j`, `k` or `g`, which navigate.
  - `confirm`: optional question to ask first. Use it on anything destructive.

## Reading the selected row

An action receives the row in its environment. Never build it into the command
any other way.

- `HIVE_ROW_<KEY>` is one field, upper-cased, with every character other than
  letters and digits replaced by `_`. Field `Names` is `$HIVE_ROW_NAMES`.
- `HIVE_ROW` is the whole row as JSON, with every value as a string.
- `HIVE_APPLET_DIR` is the applet's directory, also set for the data command.

Always double-quote these: `docker restart "$HIVE_ROW_ID"`.

## Check your work

1. Run the data command yourself and confirm its output parses and has the keys your columns name. For an html applet, run the commands the page uses.
2. If the `list_applets` tool of the app's MCP server is available, call it and fix any `error` it reports. Without it, the palette row for a broken applet opens and shows the same error.
3. Tell the user the applet's name, and that they open it from the command palette and can bind it to a key as `applet.<id>`.

## HTML applets

An `html` applet takes `version`, `title`, `view`, and optionally `icon`, `cwd`,
`entry` (the page, default `index.html`), and `width` and `height` in pixels (default
fills the window). It takes no `data`, `columns` or `actions`.

The page is a normal web page, served from the applet's directory: relative
scripts, ES modules, stylesheets, images, audio and network requests all work. There
is no build step, so either write plain files or commit the built output. Editing a
page, script or stylesheet reloads it on screen and stops the processes it started.

`window.hive` exists before your scripts run. Every call returns a promise.

- `hive.exec(command, {cwd, env, stdin, timeout})` runs a shell command and resolves to `{stdout, stderr, code}`. A non-zero `code` does not reject. `timeout` is in milliseconds and defaults to 5 minutes.
- `hive.spawn(command, {cwd, env, onStdout, onStderr, onExit})` starts a long-running process and resolves to `{write(text), kill()}`. Output arrives as text in arbitrary chunks. Closing the applet stops it.
- `hive.readFile(path)` and `hive.writeFile(path, text)` handle text files. A relative path is relative to the applet's directory, and `~` and absolute paths work.
- `hive.terminal(command, {cwd})` opens the command in the pop-up terminal.
- `hive.toast(message, {severity, body})` shows a Hive toast. `severity` is `info`, `success`, `warning`, or `error`.
- `hive.context()` resolves to `{id, title, dir, cwd, sessionSlug}`.
- `hive.close()` closes the applet.

Commands run through `sh -c` in the on-screen session's checkout when there is one,
otherwise in the applet's directory, with `$HIVE_APPLET_DIR` set.

`localStorage` works and persists per applet. Cookies and IndexedDB do not, because
the frame has an opaque origin. Read and write files for anything larger. A page can
navigate to another HTML file of its own, but cannot `fetch` one. If the frame
navigates to any other site, that page gets no `window.hive`.

Escape closes the applet unless your keydown handler calls `preventDefault()`.
Audio needs a user gesture first, as on any page.

